# November 24, 2026: the District of Columbia again, region dc, claimed with tools/choose.py --thinnest (50 places before).
# A third scatter: thirteen buildings the first two batches left out, one or two from each part of the city: Lafayette
# Square (Decatur House, St. John's), the Mall (the Smithsonian Castle), Judiciary Square (the Pension Building), Foggy
# Bottom (the Old Naval Observatory), Dupont Circle (the Heurich mansion), Georgetown (Halcyon House), Cleveland Park (the
# Peirce-Klingle mansion in Rock Creek Park), Capitol Hill (the Belmont-Paul house), NoMa (the Uline Arena), Southwest
# (Wheat Row), Anacostia (the St. Elizabeths Center Building) and Deanwood (the Strand Theater).
# Facts come from Wikipedia read as raw text (wiki_raw.py) and, for hours, from the page of whoever runs the place (the
# National Park Service, the National Building Museum, the White House Historical Association, St. John's, the Heurich
# House Museum), read with page.py. Wikipedia's lists of National Register listings gave the starting points.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'dc_mwhq','label':'Washington, D.C.: a third scatter, Lafayette Square to Deanwood','added':'2026-11-24 10:34'}
PLACES=[
N('decatur-house-dc','Decatur House','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.90006,-77.03817,'exact','open','walk','','1818',
  'A Federal-style town house on the corner of Lafayette Square, designed by Benjamin Henry Latrobe for the naval hero Stephen Decatur Jr. and finished in 1818. Decatur died in 1820. Henry Clay, Martin Van Buren and Edward Livingston later rented it while they were Secretary of State, and the Gadsby and Beale families lived in it after them. It is now a house museum run by the White House Historical Association.',
  'A house facing the square, one of only three surviving houses Latrobe designed in the country. Behind it stands a two-story service wing built by John Gadsby about 1836 as a kitchen and as quarters for the people he held in slavery; Wikipedia calls it one of the few surviving pieces of physical evidence of slavery within sight of the White House. The inside is shown on the free Monday tours. What survives of the interior furnishings was not checked.',
  'Latrobe\'s house on the square, with the slave quarters still behind it.',
  accn='Owned and run by the White House Historical Association, at the north-west corner of Lafayette Square (748 Jackson Place NW on Wikipedia\'s account). The Association\'s page offers free public tours on Mondays at 10:30 a.m. and 1 p.m., except federal holidays and the Monday after Thanksgiving; the page was read in November 2026. Parking was not checked.',
  hours='Free tours Mondays 10:30 a.m. and 1 p.m., except federal holidays and the Monday after Thanksgiving',
  links=[W('Decatur House'),['White House Historical Association: tour Decatur House','https://whitehousehistory.org/events/tour-the-historic-decatur-house']],
  brick='?'),
N('st-johns-lafayette-square-dc',"St. John's Episcopal Church, Lafayette Square",'Washington','dc','worship','standing',38.90039,-77.03609,'exact','open','walk','','1816',
  'The "Church of the Presidents", across Lafayette Square from the White House. Benjamin Henry Latrobe designed it as a Greek cross in stucco-covered brick, and it opened in 1816. Wikipedia says every sitting president since James Madison has worshipped there at least once, and Madison chose pew 28 as the president\'s pew.',
  'The Greek-cross building a Latrobe design that was changed inside after 1816: Wikipedia says the original box and high-back pews and the wine-glass pulpit were removed and most of the windows were later filled with stained glass given in memory of members. The church holds its services and free Sunday tours; what else survives of the 1816 interior was not checked.',
  'A president\'s pew in a Latrobe church.',
  accn='A working Episcopal parish, 1525 H Street NW. Its own pages (read in November 2026) give Sunday worship at 8, 9 and 11 a.m. and free tours of the church and Parish House on Sundays after the 11 a.m. service; a day-to-day visiting schedule was not found. Parking was not checked.',
  hours='Sunday worship 8, 9 and 11 a.m.; free tour of church and Parish House after the 11 a.m. service',
  links=[W("St. John's Episcopal Church, Lafayette Square"),['St. John\'s Church: tours','https://stjohns-dc.org/history/tours']],
  brick='no'),
N('smithsonian-castle-dc','Smithsonian Institution Building (the Castle)','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.88875,-77.026,'exact','view','walk','','1847–1855',
  'The first Smithsonian building, a Norman-revival "castle" by James Renwick Jr. finished in 1855. It is faced with red sandstone from the Seneca quarry in Montgomery County, Maryland, chosen because it was cheaper than marble or granite and hardens in the weather. It was named a National Historic Landmark in 1965.',
  'A red sandstone building with towers and a long front on the north side of the Mall, set apart in colour from the white and grey buildings round it. The Castle closed in February 2023 for a renovation planned to last five years, which is to restore the Great Hall to its original look. Wikipedia says it reopened for a time in May 2026 for the nation\'s 250th year and that work resumed that September.',
  'Washington\'s red sandstone castle, shut for rebuilding.',
  accn='Owned by the Smithsonian Institution on the National Mall (1000 Jefferson Drive SW). The outside can be seen from the Mall any time; the inside is closed for the renovation, and the Smithsonian\'s own page for it would not open (HTTP 403), so no hours were read and none are given here.',
  links=[W('Smithsonian Institution Building')],
  brick='no'),
N('pension-building-national-building-museum','Pension Building (National Building Museum)','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.8975,-77.01806,'exact','open','walk','','1887',
  'The former Pension Bureau, designed by the army engineer Montgomery C. Meigs and finished in 1887. Wikipedia says it took more than 15 million bricks, and the city nicknamed it "Meigs\'s Old Red Barn". It was built to give clerks air before air conditioning: every office has an outside window and opens on to a huge central court. Congress made it the National Building Museum in 1980.',
  'A red brick block with a frieze by Caspar Buberl running around the outside, 1,200 feet long and showing a parade of Civil War soldiers, and inside a Great Hall with tall columns that Wikipedia says are brick painted to look like marble. The stairs have low steps because they were made for aging and disabled veterans. The Great Hall and shop are free to enter; the museum offers free docent tours of the historic building.',
  'A brick palace for pensions, with a frieze of soldiers around it.',
  accn='Run by the National Building Museum, 401 F Street NW. Its own page, read in November 2026, gives the days and times below, says the Great Hall and shop are always free, and says docent tours are free but should be confirmed by phone. Metered street parking is on 5th, F and G Streets NW.',
  hours='Thursday to Monday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Tuesday and Wednesday, Thanksgiving and Christmas; Great Hall free',
  links=[W('National Building Museum'),['National Building Museum: visit','https://www.nbm.org/visit/']],
  brick='yes'),
N('old-naval-observatory-dc','Old Naval Observatory','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.8951,-77.05144,'exact','view','walk','','1843',
  'A Navy observatory built in 1843 on the hill above the Potomac in what is now Foggy Bottom, to rival the observatories of Europe. The Navy closed it in 1893 in favor of a new observatory, and the hill later held the Naval Medical School and hospital. It was named a National Historic Landmark in 1965.',
  'The small original observatory building still stands on Navy Hill. Wikipedia says the grounds are closed to the public and that the whole hill has passed to the State Department, with the Navy keeping some buildings. Whether anything can be seen from the street was not checked.',
  'A Navy observatory on a hill closed to the public.',
  accn='On Navy Hill (the former Potomac Annex), now State Department ground; the grounds are closed to the public, so view only if it can be seen from outside the fence. Not checked on the ground.',
  links=[W('Old Naval Observatory')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('heurich-house-dc','Heurich House Museum (the Brewmaster\'s Castle)','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.90806,-77.04444,'exact','open','walk','','1892–1894',
  'A mansion built in 1892 to 1894 for the German immigrant brewer Christian Heurich and designed by John Granville Meyers. A historical society used it until 2003, when it left and the house was put on the market. It is now a museum open to the public.',
  'Wikipedia says the first two floors are preserved and still hold most of the original furnishings, and Heurich\'s wife Mathilde worked closely with the interior designers, the Huber Brothers of New York. The house is shown only on ticketed guided tours.',
  'A brewer\'s castle with its furniture still in it.',
  accn='Run by the Heurich House Museum at 1307 New Hampshire Avenue NW. The museum\'s tours page (read in November 2026) says the mansion is shown only on guided tours of at most 10 people, each leaving at its listed start time with no late entry, booked from the dates and times on that page; it was offering no private tours then. Other sites list different days, so the days are not given here. Parking was not checked.',
  hours='Guided tours only, ticketed, 10 people a tour; dates and times are on the museum\'s tours page',
  links=[W('Heurich House Museum'),['Heurich House Museum: tours','https://heurichhouse.org/pages/tours']],
  brick='?'),
N('halcyon-house-georgetown','Halcyon House','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.90575,-77.06806,'exact','view','walk','','1787',
  'A Georgetown house begun in 1787 by Benjamin Stoddert, the first Secretary of the Navy. Albert Clemens, a nephew of Mark Twain, bought it in 1900 and spent decades rebuilding it, adding rooms inside rooms and a small crypt, because he believed rebuilding would prolong his life. Georgetown University used it as a dormitory from 1961, and it was rebuilt from 1978 to 1995 to look as it first did.',
  'Wikipedia says the restoration of 1978 to 1995 returned it to its original look. It was the headquarters of a non-profit, Halcyon, from 2014 to 2026 and was bought in 2026 by the owner of the Washington Commanders, who plans to use it as a family house. The inside is not open and what survives of Clemens\'s additions was not checked.',
  'A house a man kept rebuilding so he would not die.',
  accn='Private; look from the public sidewalk. Wikipedia\'s 2026 note says it is to be a single-family home. Parking was not checked.',
  links=[W('Halcyon House')],
  brick='?'),
N('peirce-klingle-mansion-dc','Peirce-Klingle Mansion (Linnaean Hill)','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.936,-77.05008,'exact','view','walk','','1823',
  'A farmhouse on a hill in Rock Creek Park, built in 1823 by the horticulturist Joshua Peirce, who called the place Linnaean Hill after Linnaeus and sold the first ornamental plants to the White House and the Capitol. The government bought the land in 1890 for Rock Creek Park. The Public Works Administration paid for a restoration in 1937, and the National Park Service has used the house since 1972.',
  'A three-story Pennsylvania-Dutch-style house of blue and grey granite, with a west addition of 1843, a beehive oven in the wall and a two-story stone and wood barn and carriage house to the east. Wikipedia says the poplar paneling in the stairwell and most of one mantelpiece are original. The house is a park office and is not open to visitors.',
  'A granite farmhouse that supplied the White House gardens.',
  accn='Owned by the National Park Service as part of Rock Creek Park and used as the park headquarters; the Park Service\'s listing says it is not open to the public. Look from the grounds. The nearest mapped Rock Creek Park lot is on Williamsburg Lane NW, close by to the south-west (not checked on the ground).',
  links=[W('Peirce-Klingle Mansion'),['NPS: Rock Creek Park headquarters, Klingle Mansion','https://www.nps.gov/places/000/rock-creek-park-headquarters-klingle-mansion.htm']],
  th=[38.93657,-77.05099],thn='Rock Creek Park lot on Williamsburg Lane',brick='no'),
N('belmont-paul-house-dc','Belmont-Paul Women\'s Equality National Monument (Sewall-Belmont House)','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.89233,-77.00386,'exact','open','walk','','1800',
  'A brick house finished in 1800 in the Adam Federal style on what is now Capitol Hill, owned in turn by the Sewall and Belmont families. The National Woman\'s Party bought it in 1929 and made it its headquarters. It was rescued from condemnation when the Senate wanted its ground for the Dirksen building, and Congress made it a National Park Service site.',
  'Brick laid mainly in Flemish bond, with stone lintels carrying a circular relief and a round brick arch over the doorway. In the garden the Party paved a patio with bricks saved from the Old Brick Capitol, which stood on this ground before it was torn down. The house holds exhibits on the women\'s suffrage and equal rights movements and was closed in 2022 for repair and reopened in August 2023.',
  'The suffragists\' headquarters, paved with bricks from the old Capitol.',
  accn='A National Park Service site on Constitution Avenue NE; the Park Service lists the entrance on Second Street NE. The Park Service page (read in November 2026) says it is free, open Friday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., with ranger tours at 10 a.m. and 2 p.m. An older Park Service article gave Wednesday to Sunday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; the newer page is used. Parking was not checked.',
  hours='Free, Friday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; ranger tours at 10 a.m. and 2 p.m.',
  links=[W('Belmont–Paul Women\'s Equality National Monument'),['NPS: Belmont-Paul hours and things to do','https://www.nps.gov/bepa/planyourvisit/things2do.htm']],
  brick='?'),
N('uline-arena-washington-coliseum','Uline Arena (Washington Coliseum)','Washington','dc','amuse','standing',38.905,-77.00306,'exact','view','walk','','1941',
  'A concrete ice arena opened in January 1941 by the ice magnate Michael Uline, later called the Washington Coliseum. It held the first United States concert by the Beatles on February 11, 1964, and a Rolling Stones concert in 1965. The arena was closed in 1985, then stood empty with shattered windows and graffiti and was used as a parking garage.',
  'A large concrete arena that Wikipedia says was described when new as a "concrete cavern", together with the M.J. Uline Ice Company\'s 1931 ice house on the same site. Wikipedia says the site was developed and the ice house now holds offices and an REI store; what the arena itself is used for now was not found, and the Wikipedia article says only that the arena still stands.',
  'The Beatles\' first American concert hall, still standing.',
  accn='Private (a developer, Douglas Development, bought it in 2004 per Wikipedia). View from the public streets. The nearest mapped parking is on 3rd and L Streets NE (not checked). Whether the arena is open for any event was not checked.',
  links=[W('Uline Arena')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('wheat-row-dc','Wheat Row','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.87389,-77.01806,'exact','view','walk','','1794–1795',
  'Four brick town houses on 4th Street SW, begun in 1794 and finished in 1795, among the oldest homes in the District and probably the first houses built after Washington was chosen as the capital. Wikipedia says they were poorly built of cheap materials. When the rest of Southwest was cleared in the 1950s and 60s they were saved and made part of the Harbour Square development, finished in 1964.',
  'A red brick row on stone foundations with a stone-trimmed pediment. Wikipedia says the walls are in Flemish bond except one section in English bond, thought to be the only English bond in a building of the city\'s early decades, and that the woodwork was first painted white against the red. It stands among 1960s towers, and the Thomas Law House is next door.',
  'The oldest row of houses left in Southwest.',
  accn='Private homes in the Harbour Square development, on 4th Street SW; look from the sidewalk. Parking was not checked.',
  links=[W('Wheat Row')],
  brick='yes'),
N('st-elizabeths-center-building-dc','St. Elizabeths Hospital, Center Building and West Campus','Washington','dc','inst','standing',38.85466,-76.99982,'site','view','drive','','1852–1855',
  'The first federally run psychiatric hospital in the United States, opened in 1855 as the Government Hospital for the Insane after a campaign by Dorothea Dix. The campus was named a National Historic Landmark in 1990. The Center Building was put up in three phases, west wing first, and housed the administration and the superintendent\'s home. During the Civil War the West Lodge served as a general hospital for the army.',
  'Wikipedia says the West Campus was handed to the Department of Homeland Security for its headquarters and that much of it remains empty, and that a request in late 2025 to demolish some of its buildings met objections from preservationists. The 2025 walking tours by the General Services Administration were once offered monthly and its site calls tours limited by construction and security. Which buildings still stand today was not checked.',
  'The first federal asylum, much of it still empty on its hill.',
  accn='The West Campus belongs to the federal government (the General Services Administration, with the Department of Homeland Security as tenant). It is not open without a reserved tour, and a 2026 tour schedule was not found, so treat it as view only from the public road. The pin is on the long central building of the old West Campus as it shows on the aerial. Parking was not checked.',
  links=[W('St. Elizabeths Hospital'),['GSA: St. Elizabeths West Campus','https://www.gsa.gov/real-estate/gsa-properties/ongoing-construction-projects/st-elizabeths-west-campus']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('strand-theater-deanwood-dc','Strand Theater, Deanwood','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.89819,-76.92603,'exact','view','walk','','1928',
  'A neighborhood movie house in Deanwood, opened on November 3, 1928 by Abe Lichtman. The city bought it out of foreclosure in 2006 for $230,000 and the D.C. Preservation League named it an endangered place in 2007. It was added to the National Register in 2008, and several redevelopment plans followed.',
  'Wikipedia calls it an abandoned movie theater; other sources give conflicting accounts. Cinema Treasures says that in 2021 it was gutted and turned into housing, and a restaurant in the building, Smokehouse at the Strand, opened in December 2024 and has since closed over unpaid rent. The state of the building today was not confirmed, so check the facade from the street before going.',
  'A 1928 neighborhood cinema waiting for a new use.',
  accn='On Nannie Helen Burroughs Avenue NE (5129-5131), in a commercial street; look from the sidewalk. Not open to the public as a theater. Mapped parking lies on Division Avenue NE about 50 m east (not checked).',
  links=[W('Strand Theater (Washington, D.C.)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
]
